Koi-DOS LICENSE-MANIFEST
Manifest version: 3
For: MIT (see LICENSE)

Almost all of this is MIT, which needs no manifest to explain. This file
exists for the part that is not: material written by somebody else, under
somebody else's terms, which travels inside this repository and inside the
binaries it builds.

A permissive license is not a reason to be casual. MIT, BSD, Apache-2.0 and
OFL all permit bundling into software that is sold, and every one of them
requires the notice to be kept. Dropping a notice is the one way to get a
permissive license wrong.


HOW TO READ THIS
=====================================================================

Every file has exactly one license. Where a path names a directory it covers
everything inside it; where two entries could both apply, the more specific
one wins.

A file that would need two licenses is split into two files instead. That
happened once already and is why kernel/font.c and kernel/font_glyphs.c are
separate: one holds a font somebody else drew, the other holds the code that
looks glyphs up in it. Splitting them was cheaper than an exception to the
rule, and an exception to the rule is how a manifest stops being true.


