Build output and disk images. They hold the same material as the sources
they were built from and carry the same licenses; they are listed only so
that "everything else is MIT" does not read as a claim about a 64 MB image
that also contains a firmware blob.

OVMF firmware, GCC, mtools and QEMU are used to build and run Koi-DOS and
are not distributed with it. They keep their own licenses and are nothing to
do with this file.

WAD files for K-DOOM are in no repository, here or anywhere else. The game
code is GPL; the game data is not ours to move.


WHEN SOMETHING NEW ARRIVES
=====================================================================

Third-party material is listed as:

    path/to/thing        LICENSE: <the actual license, by name>

and its license text travels with it in third-party/. "Third-party" on its
own is not an answer to "may I use this", so an entry without a named
license is a bug in this file.


HISTORY
=====================================================================

Koi-DOS was under the Koi-DOS Non-Commercial License (KNCL) through v1.4,
which was source-available, forbade commercial use, and reserved to the
maintainer the right to license the whole work commercially.

It is MIT from this commit onward. Copies obtained under KNCL keep the terms
they were obtained under, and nobody is worse off for it: every permission
KNCL gave, MIT also gives, and MIT gives more.

The change needed nobody's agreement because of the grant in Section 10 of
KNCL and the sign-off in CONTRIBUTING.md. Those existed so that the license
could be changed later, and this is the change they were for. Version 2 of
this manifest, with its base classifications and its additional permissions,
described a license that no longer applies and has been replaced rather than
edited: a manifest that half describes the old terms is worse than none.
